<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">George Berkeley</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1685">1685</a>–1753</span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Bishop Berkeley</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">George Berkeley was an Irish philosopher and Anglican <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> of Cloyne, educated at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trinity-college-dublin">Trinity College Dublin</a>. In works such as A Treatise Concerning the Principles of Human Knowledge (1710) he argued for immaterialism, the view that physical objects exist only as perceived. He also wrote on vision, mathematics and, in The Querist, on the economic problems of Ireland.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/george-berkeley.jpg" alt="George Berkeley" width="320" height="432" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: John Smibert · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:John_Smibert_-_Bishop_George_Berkeley_-_Google_Art_Project_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Berkeley was born at his family home, Dysart Castle, near Thomastown, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/county-kilkenny">County Kilkenny</a>, the eldest son of William Berkeley, a cadet of a noble family that had been feudal lords and landowners in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gloucestershire">Gloucestershire</a>. He was educated at Kilkenny College and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trinity-college-dublin">Trinity College Dublin</a>, was elected a Scholar in 1702 and took his BA in 1704. He received his MA and a Junior Fellowship in 1707 and stayed on at Trinity as librarian, lecturer in Greek and preacher. His earliest publication was on mathematics, but the first to bring him notice was An Essay towards a New Theory of Vision, of 1709.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Shortly afterwards Berkeley visited <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> and was received into the circle of Addison, Pope and Steele. Between 1714 and 1720 he interspersed academic work with travel in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, including one of the most extensive Grand Tours of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> ever undertaken. In 1721 he took holy orders in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/church-of-ireland">Church of Ireland</a> and a doctorate in divinity, and he was made Dean of Dromore soon afterwards and Dean of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/derry">Derry</a> in 1724. On 1 August 1728 he married Anne Forster and went to America, landing near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/newport-rhode-island">Newport, Rhode Island</a>, where he bought a plantation at Middletown. He purchased several enslaved Africans to work it.</p><p>He was consecrated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a> of Cloyne on 19 May 1734 and held the see until his death. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> he took part in efforts to create a home for abandoned children, and he is listed as an original governor of the Foundling Hospital, founded in 1739. His last books were Siris (1744), on the virtues of tar-water, which sold more copies in his lifetime than any other of his works, and Further Thoughts on Tar-water (1752). He retired in 1752 and went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxford">Oxford</a> to supervise the education of his son George, died on 14 January 1753 and was buried in Christ Church Cathedral.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>Berkeley held that there are only two kinds of things, spirits and ideas. Spirits are simple, active beings that produce and perceive ideas, and ideas are passive. Familiar objects such as tables and chairs are ideas perceived by the mind and cannot exist without being perceived. He believed God to be the immediate cause of all our experiences. After the poor reception of the Treatise of 1710 he rewrote it in dialogue form as Three Dialogues Between Hylas and Philonous in 1713, in which Philonous speaks for him and Hylas stands for his opponents, in particular <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-locke">John Locke</a>. In De Motu of 1721 he argued against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isaac-newton">Isaac Newton</a>'s absolute space, time and motion, and in The Analyst of 1734 he criticised the foundations of calculus.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “George Berkeley” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Protestant Ireland produced an intellectual culture of its own: Trinity College Dublin was founded in 1592, and Jonathan Swift, <b>George Berkeley</b>, Oliver Goldsmith and Edmund Burke were among its writers and thinkers.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-ireland">Kingdom of Ireland · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Dysart Castle, in what is now <a href="/countries/ireland">Ireland</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxford">Oxford</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=52.5,-7.12&amp;year=1729" aria-label="Dysart Castle on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of George Berkeley"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4630 5619h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4630 5619h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=52.5,-7.12&amp;year=1729">What was here in 1729? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Their ESSE is PERCIPI, nor is it possible they should have any existence out of the minds or thinking things which perceive them.&#8221;</p><footer>A Treatise Concerning the Principles of Human Knowledge (1710), section 3 &#183; original &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4723/pg4723-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;…the far greater part, if not all, of those difficulties which have hitherto amused philosophers, and blocked up the way to knowledge, are entirely owing to ourselves--that we have first raised a dust and then complain we cannot see.&#8221;</p><footer>A Treatise Concerning the Principles of Human Knowledge (1710), introduction &#183; original &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4723/pg4723-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;…all the choir of heaven and furniture of the earth, in a word all those bodies which compose the mighty frame of the world, have not any subsistence without a mind…&#8221;</p><footer>A Treatise Concerning the Principles of Human Knowledge (1710), section 6 &#183; original &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4723/pg4723-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 595 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
